<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">

  <!--
    Declares the privilege level LuaTools runs at. This is DOCUMENTATION, not a behaviour change: with no
    manifest the app already ran as asInvoker. Stating it explicitly means the level is a reviewed decision
    rather than a default, and it stops a future build from silently acquiring elevation.

    asInvoker is deliberate:
      * The app must NOT auto-elevate. It runs continuously (tray, HTTP bridge on 127.0.0.1:6767, CDP
        injector) and is launched automatically when Steam opens; an elevated always-on process with a
        local HTTP listener is a much larger target than the same process running as the user.
      * Everything the app does in the normal path is per-user: %AppData%, HKCU protocol registration,
        the Steam userdata/config tree.
      * The operations that CAN need admin (writing loader DLLs into a Steam installed under Program Files)
        are occasional. Those report a clear "run as administrator" message instead — see
        UnlockerService.InstallAsync. Elevating the whole app for them would be disproportionate.

    No requestedPrivileges beyond this, no uiAccess, and no self-elevation anywhere in the codebase.
  -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

</assembly>
